// Split schedule for example 02-split-add — the load-bearing
// schedule for this example.
//
// Two workers:
//   host  -- runs the effectful I/O kernels (load_input, load_input_b,
//            save_output). Owns the file system.
//   w0    -- runs the pure `add` kernel; the loop body executes here.
//
// Cross-worker dataflow edges (host <-> w0), one `transfer` per
// crossing data symbol (PRD §6.3.4: omitting a required transfer is
// a hard error, not a silent default).
//
//   a (host -> w0)   produced by load_input  on host,
//                    consumed by add         on w0.
//   b (host -> w0)   produced by load_input_b on host,
//                    consumed by add          on w0.
//   c (w0 -> host)   produced by add          on w0,
//                    consumed by save_output  on host.
//
// All three transfers are `sync`: producer blocks until consumer has
// received. This is the minimum semantic the pthreads-sync backend
// can offer (PRD §7.1 capabilities matrix). Buffering and async land
// in later schedules of later examples.
//
// What's intentionally NOT here
// -----------------------------
//   - No loop options (no `block=`, no `vectorize=`, no `pipeline=`).
//     The split is across worker boundaries only.
//   - No `notify=`, no `buffer=N`. `sync` alone, with default notify.
//   - No distributed placement (`place add on { w0, w1, ... }`). A
//     single `w0` runs the whole loop here; distributing across many
//     workers gets its own example.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0 };

    // I/O on host (owns the file system).
    place load_input    on host;
    place load_input_b  on host;
    place save_output   on host;

    // Compute on w0.
    place add           on w0;

    // Cross-worker data — one transfer per data symbol that crosses.
    transfer a : sync;
    transfer b : sync;
    transfer c : sync;
}
